[s3;:1: 1. Interactive Architecture and U`+`+ Integration&]
[s5; To elevate a simple rendering window into a professional tool, 
we must rethink how we handle user input and screen updates. 
Direct manipulation often feels rigid, so modern applications 
rely on state interpolation and decoupled rendering.&]
[s22;:1`.1: 1.1. Separation of State and Render&]
[s5;%- Directly linking mouse movement to camera rotation results 
in jerky, pixel`-perfect stuttering. Instead, we implement an 
easing pattern where the mouse and keyboard only update target 
variables like [C@5 goalx], [C@5 goaly], and [C@5 targetzoom]. &]
[s5;i150;O0;%- A U`+`+ timer runs constantly in the background, smoothly 
interpolating the actual camera position toward these goals using 
the formula [C@5 current`_value `= current`_value `+ (target`_value 
`- current`_value) `* easing`_factor], where the easing factor 
is set to [C@5 0.15]. This creates an asymptotic curve where the 
camera moves quickly at first and then gently decelerates as 
it approaches the goal. &]
[s5;i150;O0;%- Using [C^topic`:`/`/CtrlCore`/src`/Ctrl`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:Ctrl`:`:SetTimeCallback`(int`,Function`,int`)^ S
etTimeCallback()] is ideal for this because the negative interval 
tells the U`+`+ event loop to fire the callback approximately 
every 16 milliseconds to target roughly 60 frames per second 
while yielding to the operating system if the CPU is busy, preventing 
the UI thread from locking up. Furthermore, calling [C^topic`:`/`/CtrlCore`/src`/Ctrl`_en`-us`#Ctrl`:`:Refresh`(`)^ R
efresh()][C  ]inside this callback merely invalidates the window 
region, queuing a single paint event rather than forcing synchronous, 
blocking redraws.&]
[s22;:1`.2: 1.2. 2D Compositing&]
[s5;%- Drawing crisp text in pure OpenGL is notoriously painful, 
requiring texture atlases and font rasterization libraries. [C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:SculptorCtrl^ S
culptorCtrl] bypasses this entirely by integrating natively with 
U`+`+`'s [C^topic`:`/`/Painter`/src`/BufferPainter`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:BufferPainter^ I
magePainter]. You draw your UI using standard 2D vector graphics 
(software rasterized by U`+`+). [^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:AddOverlay`(const Point`&`,const Image`&`)^ G
LScene`::][C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:AddOverlay`(const Point`&`,const Image`&`)^ A
ddOverlay()] then takes this rasterized [C^topic`:`/`/Draw`/src`/Image`_en`-us`#Image`:`:class^ I
mage], generates an OpenGL texture on the fly, constructs a 2D 
orthographic quad (a plane), and composites it over your 3D scene 
with perfect alpha blending.&]
[s3;:2: 2. The Mathematics of the Illusion&]
[s5; Our custom shaders elevate the standard 3D model into an exploratory 
tool using a few clever mathematical calculations.&]
[s22;:2`.1: 2.1. Aspect`-Corrected Mouse X`-Ray&]
[s5;%- To create a magnifying glass effect that cuts away the outer 
shell of the model, we calculate the distance between the current 
pixel coordinate [C@5 fragUV ]and the mouse cursor [C@5 pointer]. 
Because most screens are rectangular rather than square, standard 
Euclidean distance checking results in an oval hole instead of 
a circular one because screen pixels are wider than they are 
tall relative to normalized UV space.&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- To fix this, we compute the screen aspect ratio as 
[C@5 uResolution.x / uResolution.y] and scale both [C@5 fragUV.x] 
and [C@5 pointer.x] by this factor before evaluation.&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- We then compute [C@5 dist `= distance(fragUV, pointer)][C .]&]
[s5;i150;O0;%-  If this distance is less than [C@5 0.15] and [C@5 gl`_FrontFacing 
]is true, we call [C@5 discard][C .]&]
[s22;:2`.2:%- 2.2. Procedural Triplanar Mapping&]
[s5;%- Standard models require explicit texture coordinates, but 
if a model lacks them, we can project a procedural pattern onto 
it from three dimensions simultaneously using [C@5 vNorm][@5  ]and 
[C@5 vPos].&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- By examining the absolute values of the normalized 
normal vector via [C@5 vec3 n `= abs(normalize(vNorm))], we select 
our projection plane.&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- If [C@5 n.x] is the largest component, the face points 
primarily along the X axis, so we assign our two`-dimensional 
projection coordinates as [C@5 uv `= vPos.yz].&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- If [C@5 n.y] is the largest component, we project along 
the Y axis using [C@5 uv `= vPos.xz], and otherwise we project 
along the Z axis using [C@5 uv `= vPos.xy].&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- Once we have our projection coordinates, we generate 
a checkerboard grid using [C@5 mod(floor(uv.x `* 2.0) `+ floor(uv.y 
`* 2.0), 2.0)].&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- If the resulting value is less than [C@5 0.5][C  ]we select 
our dark color; otherwise, we select our light color.&]
[s22;:2`.3:%- 2.3. Double`-Sided Cross`-Sections and Rim Lighting&]
[s5;%- Normally back`-faces are culled by the rendering pipeline, 
but by selectively disabling culling via [^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:Cull`(bool`)^ G
LScene`::][C^topic`:`/`/SculptorCtrl`/src`/Upp`_SculptorCtrl`_GLScene`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:GLScene`:`:Cull`(bool`)^ C
ull()], we render the interior of the model. &]
[s5;i150;O0;%- When [C@5 gl`_FrontFacing][@5  ]is false, we invert the 
normal vector using [C@5 norm `= `-norm] so internal surfaces correctly 
calculate light angles, and we darken [C@5 base.rgb] to simulate 
a solid cutaway cross`-section.&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- To highlight silhouette edges, we compute [C@5 viewdir 
`= normalize(`-vPos)] and calculate the dot product against our 
surface normal via [C@5 rim `= 1.0 `- clamp(dot(viewdir, norm), 
0.0, 1.0)].&]
[s5;i150;O0;%- Surfaces pointing directly toward the camera yield 
a dot product of one and produce no rim light, while grazing 
edges yield zero and receive maximum rim light. Raising this 
value via [C@5 pow(rim, 3.0)]creates a tight, glowing fringe along 
the outer contours of the model.&]
